Course Requirements: Individual Assignments 
 
Case Analysis: To prepare to engage in classroom case discussions, you will need to complete a 
1-page analysis of the assigned case before attending class. This will enable you to apply the 
concepts and theories from the assigned readings to a real-world situation. This goes beyond 
simply reading the case. You will identify the problem (s) in the case and apply the relevant 
concepts/theories to explain their underlying causes. Additional instructions and a grading rubric 
are posted on Brightspace.  
 
Personal Reflection Paper: You will write a paper that describes what you have learned about 
yourself and your capability as a leader from your experiences and learning in the course. This 
involves thoughtful reflection and insightful connection between your course experience and the 
learning or insights you have gained about yourself. Narrative accounts that convey an 
understanding of your experience supported by course material and your personal assessment 
results are helpful. Instructions and a grading rubric are posted on Brightspace. 
 
Course Requirements: Participation 
 
Class Participation and Team Engagement: This part of your grade is based on consistent and 
effective contributions to class discussions/activities and active engagement with your project team. 
You are expected to attend every class session having read, thought about, and prepared any 
assigned material. You should also be ready to share your ideas and actively listen to and interpret 
the ideas presented by others. Also, missing more than two lectures or leaving early for more than 
two lectures will affect your performance in the course unless you communicate in advance with the 
professor. Missing three or more classes without a reasonable conversation with the professor will 
result in the loss of at least a full letter grade.   
 
Comments that are vague, repetitive, unrelated to the discussion, or disrespectful of others will be 
evaluated negatively. Quality comments possess one or more of the following attributes: 
● Offer a relevant concept or personal experience. 
● Provide careful analysis by applying concepts from readings and lectures. 
● Move the discussion forward by building on previous contributions with new insights. 
● Ask thoughtful and challenging questions. 
 
Openness, respect, and active engagement are essential to our classroom learning. Class sessions 
are psychologically safe where we can all learn together and benefit from each other’s 
perspectives and experiences. Because there is rarely one right answer in organizational behavior 
and leadership, discussions are richer if you share your unique perspective.  
 
